### \f0\ltrch\lang1033 5\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Database Schema (SQL)\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 When you run `dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate` and `dotnet ef database update`, your database will have the following tables\f1\rtlch\lang1037 :\par
\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Users**: Stores user credentials, roles (manager or customer)\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Donors**: Information about donors\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Donations**: Tracks each donation made by a donor\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Gifts**: Information about each gift available for purchase\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Purchases**: Tracks the purchases made by users\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Lottery**: Contains information about lottery winners for each gift\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
```\f0\ltrch\lang1033 sql\f1\rtlch\lang1037\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Users\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 UserId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Username NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 PasswordHash NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 255\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Role NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 50\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Email NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Phone NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 50\f1\rtlch\lang1037 )\par
);\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Donors\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 DonorId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Name NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Email NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Phone NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 50\f1\rtlch\lang1037 )\par
);\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Donations\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 DonationId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Amount DECIMAL\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Date DATETIME\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 DonorId INT\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 FOREIGN KEY (DonorId) REFERENCES Donors(DonorId)\f1\rtlch\lang1037\par
);\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Gifts\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 GiftId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Name NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Category NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 50\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Price DECIMAL\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 DonorId INT\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 FOREIGN KEY (DonorId) REFERENCES Donors(DonorId)\f1\rtlch\lang1037\par
);\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Purchases\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 PurchaseId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 GiftId INT\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 CustomerId INT\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Date DATETIME\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 FOREIGN KEY (GiftId) REFERENCES Gifts(GiftId)\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 FOREIGN KEY (CustomerId) REFERENCES Users(UserId)\f1\rtlch\lang1037\par
);\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 CREATE TABLE Lottery\f1\rtlch\lang1037  (\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 LotteryId INT PRIMARY KEY\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 GiftId INT\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Winner NVARCHAR\f1\rtlch\lang1037 (\f0\ltrch\lang1033 100\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ),\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 DrawDate DATETIME\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ,\par
    \f0\ltrch\lang1033 FOREIGN KEY (GiftId) REFERENCES Gifts(GiftId)\f1\rtlch\lang1037\par
);\par
```\par
\par
### \f0\ltrch\lang1033 6\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 What the User Sees\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **\par
\par
#### \f0\ltrch\lang1033 a) **When a Customer (Client) Opens the Site\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 1\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Login Page\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **: \par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 The first screen the customer sees will be a login page if they are not logged in\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 The login form asks for `Username` and `Password`. After logging in, they are redirected to the gift list page\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 2\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Gift List Page\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **:\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 After logging in, they are shown a list of available gifts. Each gift has the following properties: Name, Category, Price\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 There will be an "Add to Cart" button next to each gift\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 Customers can select multiple gifts to add to their cart, and once they're done, they can proceed to purchase\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 3\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Purchase Page\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **:\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 When the customer clicks to buy a gift, the backend checks if the gift is still available and not already purchased\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 If available, the purchase is processed, and the customer gets a confirmation\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 4\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Lottery Results\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **:\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 After the lottery has been drawn by the admin, customers can see the winner for each gift. This is displayed on the gift list page as soon as the lottery is over\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
#### \f0\ltrch\lang1033 b) **When an Admin Opens the Site\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 1\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Login Page\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **: \par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 The admin can log in through the same login page but will have a role of "manager\f1\rtlch\lang1037 ."\par
   \par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 2\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Admin Dashboard\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **:\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 The admin is redirected to the admin dashboard where they can see all gifts and their associated lotteries\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   - \f0\ltrch\lang1033 The admin has options to manually trigger the lottery for each gift and view the winners of previous lotteries\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
   \par
### \f0\ltrch\lang1033 7\f1\rtlch\lang1037 . **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Final Remarks\f1\rtlch\lang1037 **\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 This setup covers the core functionality for managing a raffle (mimicking the "Chinese auction" or "mystery gift raffle") with two distinct user roles, customers and admins. Here's what we have\f1\rtlch\lang1037 :\par
\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Backend**: .NET Core Web API with JWT authentication, role-based access control, and full CRUD operations for managing donors, gifts, purchases, and lotteries\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Frontend**: Angular app with services for interacting with the API, displaying gift lists, handling cart functionality, and showing lottery results\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
- **\f0\ltrch\lang1033 Database**: SQL Server (or any relational database) structured to store user, donor, gift, purchase, and lottery data\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
\f0\ltrch\lang1033 By using a layered architecture and separating concerns (API, service, repository, etc.), this system is easy to maintain and expand in the future\f1\rtlch\lang1037 .\par
\par
